WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.000
Todd Phillips' new film Joker is no laughing
matter.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.380
“haha.”

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:14.010
This dark, gritty, R-rated take on DC Comics'
infamous Clown Prince of Crime isn't premiering

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:19.160
until October 4th, but the film had its debut
on Saturday, August 31st at the Venice Film

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.180
Festival in Italy.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.090
So what happened when audiences finally saw
Joaquin Phoenix portray the mentally unstable

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:26.340
comedian Arthur Fleck?

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.180
"Well no one's laughing now."

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.520
"You can say that again, pal."

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.060
Most critics agree: The film is pure magic.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:37.480
According to Deadline,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.580
"[Joker] was met with an eight-minute standing
ovation and shouts of 'Bravo!' at its conclusion."

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.510
Critics raced to their laptops to publish
their first impressions of the film, and most

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:48.580
reviewers were effusive in their praise.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.500
Right now, the critical consensus on Rotten
Tomatoes proclaims that,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.640
"Joker gives its infamous central character
a chillingly plausible origin story that serves

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.440
as a brilliant showcase for its star and a
dark evolution for comics-inspired cinema."

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.900
Writing for Vital Thrills, Jenna Busch applauded
Phoenix's transformative performance as Arthur

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:08.400
Fleck:

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.360
"The physical [movement] of the character
alone could convey what is going on with him,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.500
even if there had been no dialogue.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.890
[...] He's awkward and twisted (particularly
his arms and the position of his head) when

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:20.560
he's trying to be 'normal.'

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.290
The little dances he does as he tries to act
sexy or confident are the sort that make you

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.980
embarrassed for someone at a party.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.340
As he becomes who he really is, the movements
are powerful."

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.140
Busch gave Joker a perfect score, adding,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.660
"I'm struggling to find something to critique
here.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.780
As I've spoken about the film to myself and
my colleagues, I've come up with nothing.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.200
I'm sure I won't be the only one.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.140
This standalone film was a brave choice by
Warner Bros., and it's very likely going to

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:47.640
pay off.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.140
Again, this is a difficult film to watch,
and it's going to take time to process, but

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.860
ultimately, I think this is going to be a
definitive part of Joker canon."

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:02.340
Empire Online's Terri White also gave the
film a flawless score five out of five stars

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.040
writing that:

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.140
"[Joker is] a sad, chaotic, slow-burn study
of someone who isn't visible; who doesn't

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.880
even exist to the world around them."

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.060
Elsewhere in her review, she writes:

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.780
"What writer/director Todd Phillips and co-writer
Scott Silver[...] have written into life is

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.400
the Joker as a character.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.420
What they and the film [are] interested in
is the mental, moral, emotional, physical

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.620
make-up of the man who became the Joker.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.190
[...] Bold, devastating and utterly beautiful,
Todd Phillips and Joaquin Phoenix have not

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.900
just reimagined one of the most iconic villains
in cinema history, but reimagined the comic

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:35.940
book movie itself."

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.220
Time Out's Phil De Semlyen also gave Joker
five stars writing:

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:46.160
"This is a truly nightmarish vision of late-era
capitalism – arguably the best social horror

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.420
film since Get Out [...]."

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.740
In his review, De Semlyen also writes:

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.000
"[Phoenix] runs Heath Ledger cigarette paper-close
as the finest screen Joker."

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:00.780
Brandon Davis at ComicBook.com also gave the
film a perfect score five out of five stars.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.860
He writes that Joker is quite unlike any DC
film or comic book movie that's come before

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.360
it.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.000
In fact, he predicts the film will rake in
lots of awards, writing that:

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.200
"Phoenix's Arthur laughs his way through terrible
scenarios.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:18.920
For this, the actor demands an Oscar nomination
[and] it might not be the only nod this DC

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:19.760
Comics movie earns.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:25.120
The cinematography, score, and direction create
something unlike anything before it and it’s

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.840
terrifying, thrilling, and moving."

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.560
He also makes a point to say,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.320
"There has never been a movie like this in
the genre.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.340
[…] You'll need to see to believe it and,
even then, you still might not believe it."

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:40.700
Pete Hammond at Deadline loved the film too,
and was particularly affected by Phoenix's

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:42.120
work, writing that,

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.680
"[The actor gives] a performance so dazzling,
risky, and original, you might as well start

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.720
engraving his name on the Oscar right now."

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:49.940
He also claims,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.940
"No joke, this is a movie [...] unlike any
other from the DC universe, and you will find

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.340
it impossible to shake off.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:57.400
At least I did."

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.560
Hammond absolutely loved Phoenix's performance,
writing that:

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.690
"Heath Ledger took it in a different direction
in The Dark Knight and won a posthumous Supporting

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:10.030
Actor Oscar, and now Joaquin Phoenix gets
his turn as the character in the stand-alone

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:15.620
origin movie Joker in a bravura piece of filmmaking
that speaks to the world we are actually living

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.120
in today in ways that few movies do.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.760
If you thought you knew Joker before this
Joker, think again."

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:26.600
Many reviewers said the film offers a stark
commentary on modern society.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.740
Deadline's Pete Hammond argued that the film
will make audiences think about the role that

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:32.620
violence plays in our current culture.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.320
Of course, not everyone enjoyed Joker but
negative reviews have been few and far between.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.400
RogerEbert.com critic Glenn Kenny didn't feel
like the film offered any sort of meaningful

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:43.790
message, writing that:

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:47.570
"As social commentary, Joker is pernicious
garbage."

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:48.940
Kenny also claimed that,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:54.960
"The final minutes, which will move any sentient
viewer to mutter 'would you just pick a g------

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.370
ending and stick to it?' are likely an indication
of what kind of mess we would have had on

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:04.270
our hands had Phillips been left entirely
to his own cynical incoherent devices for

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.620
the entire runtime."

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.580
Stephanie Zacharek of TIME Magazine felt similarly:

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:13.680
"The aggressive and possibly irresponsible
idiocy of Joker overall is [Todd Phillips']

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.880
alone to answer for.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.720
Phillips may want us to think he's giving
us a movie all about the emptiness of our

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.000
culture, but really, he's just offering a
prime example of it.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:27.500
[…] Joker is dark only in a stupidly adolescent
way, but it wants us to think it's imparting

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.900
subtle political or cultural wisdom."

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.630
She also found Phoenix's performance extremely
off-putting, writing that:

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.680
"[He's] acting so hard you can feel the desperation
throbbing in his veins.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.060
He leaves you wanting to start him a GoFundMe,
so he won’t have to pour so much sweat into

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.169
his job again."

00:05:44.169 --> 00:05:49.320
Well, viewers will finally get a chance to
form their own opinions when Joker hits theaters

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.520
on October 4th and we're sure the film is
going to hit hard.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.160
"Is this a joke to you?"

